// A MANIFEST-LESS tree compiled from one entry is ONE package, so
// `public(package)` reaches across a relative import inside it.
//
// This cell was the visibility grid's one red row while DF-232n was open: with
// neither side carrying a `--module-path` name, `check_visibility` was asked
// with no package root at all and read that as "assume same package" — a
// fail-OPEN default that made the tier real only for consumers arriving through
// `--module-path` or std. DF-232n closed it by IDENTITY rather than by a
// blanket fail-closed, and the identity a manifest-less file gets is the ENTRY
// file's directory tree (`ModuleResolver.package_identity` arm 2, DF-229c's
// ruling generalized). Both files here are under `examples/module_matrix/`, so
// they are one package and the reach is CORRECT.
//
// The refusal side of the same rule lives at
// `examples/package_tier_foreign_relative_reach_error.saw`, where the callee is
// under its own `Saw.toml` (arm 1) and the entry is outside that root, and the
// same-manifest accept side at
// `examples/module_tests/pkg232n/tests/package_tier_same_package_reach.saw`.
// So all three arms an in-tree compile can take are pinned.
//
// The file this replaces asserted the OPPOSITE (`EXPECT: error`, citing
// DF-232n), and kept asserting it for two days after that finding closed — an
// xfail whose citation has closed still FAILS, so no gate looks at it. DF-248c.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 1

